The old MC@NLO samples under pp_tj_*, g_runs/ and scale_runs/ are superseded
by the 25 October 2026 normalization fix. Regenerate events and showers;
reshowering their existing LHE files does not repair their weights. From
the repository root, the complete rerun command is:

    python3 paper/run_all.py --cores 64

It prepares fresh central_runs_fixed/C_GLB, C_LOC, C_Q and C_QT exports,
plus g_runs_fixed/ and scale_runs_fixed/, then makes the comparison plots.
The completed fixed-order and POWHEG samples are reused. --dry-run lists
all 22 affected samples; --prepare-only exports without integration.
--cores controls integration and parallel shower workers (default 64).
Each million-event shower splits into 64 jobs with distinct seeds and
combines their histograms and scale weights into the usual output files.

Within a fresh central process, the usual MG5_aMC shower command still works.
For example, from central_runs_fixed/C_LOC/ after generating C_LOC:

    ./bin/shower C_LOC -f

Cards/shower_card.dat requests all one million events, in 64 shower jobs.
Campaign completion records also protect the phase-space geometry and FKS
sector-function sources; exports predating the numerical repairs must be
regenerated before their results can be treated as validated predictions.
The common analysis is installed in each process's MCatNLO/PY8Analyzer/.
Its master copy is Template/NLO/MCatNLO/PY8Analyzer/py8an_HwU_pp_tj_recoil.f.
After editing that copy, copy it into the four processes before showering again.
This analysis uses include/HEPMC_ST_RECOIL.INC with 100000 history entries;
PYABEG configures the same capacity in HepMC through include/HEPEVTSize.h
in the Pythia analysis driver. The stock 4000-entry record can silently
truncate long global-recoil histories and lose final particles, including
the stable top. This interface fix is installed in all four processes and
the export template. It does not change shower physics or event selection.

Outputs appear in Events/<run_id>/plot_PYTHIA8_1_0.{HwU,pdf,html,py,gnuplot}.
Subsequent showers increment the first index. The standard MG5 plotting
machinery produces PDF and HTML automatically; Matplotlib is available here.
There are 75 histograms, grouped into 37 pages in the standard PDF because
distributions with the same name overlay the ALL, TAG and GAP selections.
The HwU files retain bin-integrated cross sections and statistical errors.

To compare the two samples after showering, run from the repository root:

    python3 Analyses/compare_pp_tj_recoil.py
    python3 Analyses/compare_pp_tj_recoil.py --shape

By default this reads the fresh C_GLB and C_LOC exports. C_Q and C_QT
are selected by --patched and --two-patches; --central-dir PATH changes
their common parent. No old pp_tj_* fallback is used. The run names are
inferred per process, so C_* samples can be compared with POWHEG run_01.
Superseded process/HwU paths and symlinks to them are rejected. It creates
Analyses/pp_tj_recoil.pdf, the corresponding cross-section table in JSON,
and, with --shape, pp_tj_recoil_shapes.pdf and its JSON table. Each PDF
separates the selections and overlays local and default recoil with a
local/default ratio. The shape option normalizes over the displayed range;
it does not normalize the one-bin cross sections or gap efficiencies.
Explicit HwU paths, --run and --out are also accepted. For example:

    python3 Analyses/compare_pp_tj_recoil.py --run recoil_check --out /tmp/tj_check

There is also pp_tj_local_patch, a fresh process with the same production
and analysis settings and a private, opt-in Pythia quark IF numerator patch.
Its ./bin/generate_events -f generates events and showers them automatically.
The special outgoing-collinear G correction is replaced by the patched
kernel in both shower and matching; the ordinary soft G and its C-SC overlap
remain. See pp_tj_local_patch/README.local_patch and
docs/audits/pythia8318_local_collinear_patch.txt. After generating all three:

    python3 Analyses/compare_pp_tj_recoil.py --patched
    python3 Analyses/compare_pp_tj_recoil.py --patched --shape

The patch curve is green. --patched also accepts an explicit HwU file.

pp_tj_local_2patch adds the corresponding quark-IF massive-top soft patch
in a second private build. Its ./bin/generate_events -f uses the same cards
and analysis with both kernel patches in the shower and counterterms;
the local G replacements are then disabled. The extra factor follows the
current incoming-quark/top connection and leaves Pythia's final-final
mass treatment unchanged. See pp_tj_local_2patch/README.local_2patch.
After generating all four samples, overlay them with:

    python3 Analyses/compare_pp_tj_recoil.py --two-patches
    python3 Analyses/compare_pp_tj_recoil.py --two-patches --shape

The two-patch curve is purple. --two-patches also accepts an explicit
HwU file, and implies inclusion of the one-patch sample.

pp_tj_powheg supplies the independent five-flavour stable-top POWHEG
comparison with stock Pythia local recoil and the same analysis. From
the repository root, ./pp_tj_powheg/bin/generate_events runs the complete
generation and shower chain with 64 workers. See its README.powheg for
inputs and the standard POWHEG veto prescription. To overlay its output:

    python3 Analyses/compare_pp_tj_recoil.py --two-patches --powheg
    python3 Analyses/compare_pp_tj_recoil.py --two-patches --powheg --shape

The POWHEG curve is ochre. --powheg also accepts an explicit HwU file;
it can be used without --two-patches to compare just the two original
MC@NLO samples and POWHEG. All ratios use the default-recoil MC@NLO
sample as denominator.

The analysis clusters final-state quarks (including b) and gluons with
anti-kt, R=0.4, using FastJet or the bundled fjcore fallback. It excludes
the stable top. Jets must have pT>20 GeV and |y|<4.5. j1 and j2 mean the
leading and second accepted jets ordered by pT. No flavour or ancestry
assignment is made: j1 is a kinematic proxy for the spectator jet and can
instead be a hard radiated jet. No massless-parton flavour tag is required.

ALL has no jet requirement. It contains the total cross section, inclusive
top pT and y, |y_t|, a wider top-pT distribution, jet multiplicity, jet HT,
pT_top+HT, and leading-jet pT when a jet exists. TAG requires pT_j1>30 GeV.
GAP additionally requires |y_t-y_j1|>3 and m(t,j1)>500 GeV. These cuts are
analysis selections, not generation cuts. Both TAG and GAP contain the
standard single-top distributions: top and leading-jet pT and rapidity,
leading-jet eta and |eta|, top-jet invariant mass and pair rapidity,
rapidity separation, azimuthal separation and jet multiplicity. The last
multiplicity bins include Njets>=8 or Ngap>=5, respectively.

Recoil probes include pT(t+j1), also with fine bins below 50 GeV, pT(t+j1+j2),
j2 pT/y/eta and its position relative to the top and leading jet. Define

    dy = |y_j1-y_t|, y_mid = (y_t+y_j1)/2, s = sign(y_j1-y_t),
    zstar = s*(y_j2-y_mid)/dy.

zstar is filled only for dy>1; the top is at -1/2 and j1 at +1/2.
A second zstar distribution requires pT_j2>30 GeV. The unnormalized
y_j2-y_mid and oriented separations s*(y_j2-y_j1), s*(y_j2-y_t) are also
included. The signed distance to the nearer edge of the top-j1 gap is
positive inside the gap and negative outside it. Jet rapidity is true
rapidity; eta is pseudorapidity computed from the three-momentum.

Extra jets mean j2, j3, ... . The analysis measures their multiplicity and
scalar pT sum inside the top-j1 rapidity interval, their hardest pT there,
and their scalar pT sum in |y-y_mid|<0.5. Zero activity contributes at zero.
The two gap-veto scans are cumulative cross sections: one is the selected
reference rate at every Q0, and the other requires no extra gap jet above
Q0. Their ratio is the gap-veto efficiency, scanned at Q0=21,23,...,99 GeV.
The comparison script forms this ratio with the subset covariance, rather
than treating the numerator and denominator as independent. It preserves
signed weights and their sum of squares. Ratios between recoil samples
assume independent samples and mask nonpositive default denominators.

The VBF analogy concerns the first additional jet (j2 here, j3 in VBF).
Its centrality and gap activity are useful coherence/recoil probes, but
the coloured massive top makes this a different system; a large VBF-like
effect is not assumed. Useful references are Jaeger et al., arXiv:2003.12435
(https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.12435) and Hoeche et al., arXiv:2106.10987
(https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.10987). Top-plus-one-jet observables have the
usual NLO+PS accuracy. Additional-jet distributions have lower fixed-order
accuracy, and the recoil after adding t+j1+j2 probes further shower radiation
(also radiation outside the accepted jets). There is no jet merging.

The two shower cards share the same perturbative settings. Hadronisation,
the hadron-level stage, MPI, beam remnants, primordial kT, colour
reconnection, QED/weak showers, top decays and MECs are disabled. QCD ISR
and FSR remain on. Without beam remnants the final state is an open partonic
subsystem; Check:event=off suppresses the inapplicable full-proton charge
and momentum check. The analysis requires one stable top and rejects final
hadrons, photons and leptons. Light/charm/bottom shower masses are unchanged.

Default: SpaceShower:dipoleRecoil=off, TimeShower:globalRecoil=on (the usual
MG5 MC@NLO first-emission settings are retained). Local: dipoleRecoil=on,
globalRecoil=off, as required for the locally matched event file. Both
disable extra smooth ISR screening and hard-emission damping, while retaining
the ordinary shower cutoff. Shower alpha_s and its running are the standard
MG5 values, 0.118 and one loop, with CMW rescaling off. Both use Pythia's
internal NNPDF2.3 NLO QCD+QED PDF (pSet=15), corresponding to the hard-event
nn23nlo set; this does not require LHAPDF. The LHE maps/counterterms differ,
so this compares the two matched predictions, rather than toggling the
shower recoil on a single event sample.

Cards/amcatnlo_configuration.txt points to the existing unmodified stock
Pythia 8.318 build at /tmp/singletop_local_matching_pythia. Both cards link
HepMC2 from /export/slow1/rikkert/HepMC. If those installations move, update
pythia8_path, the card's DM_2 absolute XML/PDF-data path, and the HepMC include
and library paths. DM_1 preserves Pythia's case-sensitive lower-case "force"
keyword, which would be upper-cased inside MG5's pythia8_options dictionary.
No Pythia source has been patched.

Validation uses separate Events/recoil_check/ samples with 1000 input events
each, leaving Events/run_01/events.lhe.gz untouched. Both showers accept all
1000 events and produce PDF/HTML/HwU files. The existing MG5 driver invokes
the analysis on 999 of them because its event-limit check precedes analysis
of the last event; the effect is one event in a million for the full runs.
These short checks validate the setup, not the physical size of recoil
effects. Stock Pythia reports nonfatal local-shower threshold/dipole warnings.

Physics checks with the real HwU library and fjcore run with:

    python3 -m unittest tests.unit_tests.various.test_single_top_recoil_analysis -v

They check signed and variation weights, normalization including events
failing the jet cuts, collinear recombination, beam-reversal invariance of
the oriented rapidity coordinate, and the gap-veto threshold selections.
